CORE
main.c
--32
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
--
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^warning: ignoring
--
p points into s at an undecided offset, so p->lock = &x is a byte update of
the whole of s at a non-constant offset. The lowering binds the update value
once by a let and places the let at the innermost expressions using it (see
byte_update_struct_symbolic_offset). For a pointer member of s that
expression is pointer-typed, and bv_pointerst had no case for a let: it
reported "warning: ignoring let" and gave the member free bits. Every pointer
member of s then took an arbitrary value after the store, here the parent
links, and both assertions FAILED. The size matters: with 6 nodes (120 bytes)
no let reached a pointer member and the bug did not show; with 7 or more it
did. Found as a false alarm on libvsync/hmcslock.i (unreach-call, expected
TRUE).
